      "title": "Democratic state victories open paths to clean-energy and data-center rules",
      "content_html": "Democrats gained control of the governor’s office and both legislative chambers in five states—Michigan, Minnesota, Nevada, Pennsylvania and Wisconsin—alongside their midterm gains in Congress. Arizona could become a sixth new state-level governing trifecta as vote counting continues.\n\nWisconsin Democrats are weighing utility planning rules and clean-power requirements for data centers, while Pennsylvania lawmakers are promoting community and rooftop solar and a $1.5 billion transit rescue package. In Arizona, Democrats’ gains at the utility regulator and in the legislature could revive disputes over groundwater and energy use by data centers.\n\nPresident Donald Trump can veto legislation, and newly elected lawmakers do not take office until January. A bipartisan permitting measure is scheduled for a Senate procedural vote on Nov. 9; clean-energy advocates and some tribal groups oppose it for different reasons.",
